course: dictionary
lesson: 14140
title: Misbehaviour and what it means
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Misbehaviour is a noun, a word for a thing. It comes from misbehave, which means to behave badly. The little word mis- often means wrong or bad, so misbehaviour is bad behaviour. «Misbehaviour in class will lead to a detention.» A detention is a punishment, so misbehaviour is something children get in trouble for.
explain: Misbehaviour is not the same as a mistake. A mistake is when you get something wrong by accident. Misbehaviour is when you do something you know is not allowed. «Tom spilled his juice by accident, so it was not misbehaviour.» Spilling it on purpose to annoy his sister would be misbehaviour.
explain: In a formal text, misbehaviour can mean wrong or dishonest conduct by someone in charge, such as an official. «The inspector lost his job for misbehaviour.» Here the clue is lost his job: this is serious, not a child breaking a class rule.
fact: Misbehaviour is a noun, so you can say «the misbehaviour» or «her misbehaviour».
fact: «Misbehaviour» means bad behaviour: doing things you know are not allowed.
fact: In a formal text, «misbehaviour» can mean wrong or dishonest conduct, for example by an official.
fact: Phrases you will meet are «punished for misbehaviour» and «misbehaviour in class».
fact: In American English the spelling is «misbehavior», without the u.
exercise: «The class was calm all morning, with no misbehaviour at all.» What does misbehaviour mean here? | Making a mistake in sums | Good work and neat writing | *Bad behaviour, doing things that are not allowed | Being late for lunch
exercise: Mum says, «Your misbehaviour cost you your phone for the evening.» What was the misbehaviour? | *Doing things you know are not allowed | Losing your coat | Forgetting your homework | Eating your tea
exercise: «The referee was sacked for misbehaviour after taking money from one team.» What does the formal meaning suggest? | *Wrong or dishonest conduct by someone in charge | Winning a prize | Running round the pitch | Playing football very well
test: «Ruby was punished for misbehaviour in the dinner hall.» What does misbehaviour mean? | Being kind to her friends | Eating all her vegetables | Reading a book quietly | *Doing things that are not allowed
test: In the sentence «Misbehaviour in class will lead to a detention», which word is a clue that misbehaviour is bad? | Lead | Will | Class | *Detention
exam: «The inspector lost his job for misbehaviour.» In this sentence, misbehaviour most likely means: | Bad weather on the day of the visit | *Wrong or dishonest conduct by an official | Good manners at the office | Playing a fun game with friends
